WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.970
A route problem is defined by its rules:

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:04.060
which points are joined,

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.910
and which directions are allowed.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.370
Before counting anything, read those rules exactly.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.340
Two lines that cross in a drawing are not a junction

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.310
unless the problem says so.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.550
Tracing every route by hand is slow and you lose track.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.280
There is a method that counts all routes at once,

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.790
by writing one number on each point.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:26.950
A walker goes from the bottom-left corner to the

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.500
top-right corner of a two by two block of streets,

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.480
moving only right or up.

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:33.640
How many different routes are there?

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.970
Pause and think about how you could arrive

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:36.900
at each corner.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.350
Write on each corner the number of ways to reach it.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.500
Along the bottom row and up the left side there is only

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.760
one way: straight along.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.250
Write one on each.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.020
To reach any other corner, your last step came either

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.510
from the left or from below.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.170
So its number is the sum of those two neighbours.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.270
The middle corner gets one plus one: two.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:01.970
The next corners get three.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.430
The final corner gets three plus three: six routes.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.170
The method copes with obstacles.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.720
If the centre corner is closed, write zero there and

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:12.630
carry on adding.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.820
Now only two routes remain: all the way round one side

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:17.550
or the other.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.690
Always check what counts as a connection.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.830
If one path crosses over another on a bridge,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.150
you cannot turn there.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.290
Only the connections the rules give you are real.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.750
Read the rules exactly.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.150
Label each point with the number of ways to reach it,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.130
adding the points that lead into it.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:37.970
A blocked point gets zero.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.880
Notebook challenge: how many right-and-up routes cross a

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.490
block of streets three wide and two high?
